1
00:00:00,430 --> 00:00:03,790
The Japanese army also came with a very practical idea.

2
00:00:04,270 --> 00:00:05,770
It did not bring many bicycles.

3
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:10,670
It knew Malaya was full of them, so it took them from shops and civilians and rode

4
00:00:10,670 --> 00:00:11,070
south.

5
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:16,170
A British officer who lay hidden beside a road behind the Japanese lines later

6
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:20,110
described them cycling past in groups of 40 or 50,

7
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:25,370
3 or 4 abreast, chatting and laughing as if they were off to a football match,

8
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:29,370
which brings us to the first myth at the gate, the jungle.

9
00:00:29,830 --> 00:00:33,790
You'll often hear that the British thought Malaya's jungle was passable,

10
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:36,350
so the Japanese simply walked through it.

11
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:38,290
The truth is more awkward.

12
00:00:38,970 --> 00:00:42,510
Much of what everyone called jungle was actually rubber plantations,

13
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:45,290
coconut groves, villages, and roads.

14
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:49,050
The real jungle at the edges looked impenetrable,

15
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:53,210
and the Australian official history says that impression was misleading.

16
00:00:53,670 --> 00:00:56,370
The Japanese mostly didn't hack through Virgin Forest.

17
00:00:57,130 --> 00:01:01,890
They used the roads and the plantations, went around the British positions on the

18
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:06,070
paths beside them, and kept moving faster than the defenders could dig in.
